# Line endings.
#
# The project is developed on Windows and built on Linux, and the test suite compares
# program output byte for byte. A `\r` that appears on one machine and not the other makes
# a passing test fail for no reason, so text files are normalised in the repository and
# checked out with the platform's convention.
* text=auto

*.c      text
*.h      text
*.cpp    text
*.ax     text
*.axd    text
*.md     text
*.py     text
*.json   text
*.cmake  text
*.yml    text
*.txt    text
*.svg    text

# Windows batch and shell scripts need their own endings to run at all.
*.bat    text eol=crlf
*.cmd    text eol=crlf
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Binary assets. Without this, git treats them as text and a checkout on a machine with
# different line-ending settings silently corrupts them.
#
# `.ico` is absent on purpose: the raster icons are not tracked here (see .gitignore).
# Listing a pattern for a file type that cannot be committed only suggests it might be.
*.png    binary
*.vsix   binary
*.dll    binary
*.so     binary
*.exe    binary
*.acvm   binary

# ── GitHub language detection ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# GitHub decides a repository's language from a server-side database (github/linguist).
# These lines tell it that `.ax` and `.axd` are Axiom rather than "Other", which is what
# the language bar on the repository page reads.
#
# These lines are only half the story, and the missing half is not something a repository
# can supply. `linguist-language` resolves its value against linguist's own database
# (`Language.find_by_alias`); a name that is not there resolves to nil, and a file whose
# language is nil is counted under no language at all. It does not fall back to extension
# matching, and it does not create the language.
#
# So until Axiom is registered (see editors/linguist/), these two lines mean "count these
# files as nothing" -- which is why the language bar shows no Axiom entry. Registering it
# is a pull request to github/linguist, and the lines above start working the moment it
# merges, with no change here.
*.ax     linguist-language=Axiom linguist-detectable
*.axd    linguist-language=Axiom linguist-detectable

# `.axd` is ASP.NET's Web Handler extension, so without the line above these 10 files are
# counted as ASP.NET -- which is what the repository reported before this file existed.
# The override is what stops that, once Axiom is a registered language.

# Everything written in Axiom counts. Test programs and examples are Axiom source too --
# excluding them would describe the repository as a C++ project with a small language
# attached, which is the opposite of what it is. An earlier version of this file did
# exclude them, and the arithmetic is worth recording: with tests and examples counted,
# Axiom is the largest language at ~49% of the tree; without them it drops to ~39% and
# falls behind C++. Whether a repository's tests are part of it is a judgement call; here
# they are, because the standard library is verified by programs written in the language.
#
# Only the grammar sample is excluded, since it is a fixture for the highlighter rather
# than part of the implementation.
editors/samples/*  linguist-documentation
